===== 13. Closer look: the states that need a second pass =====
# CrashLoopBackOff only shows once the backoff is long enough that the pod
# is waiting rather than running. Checked again ~5 minutes in:

$ kubectl -n lifecycle get pod lifecycle-crashloop --no-headers
lifecycle-crashloop   0/1   CrashLoopBackOff   5 (109s ago)   4m58s

$ kubectl -n lifecycle get pod lifecycle-crashloop -o jsonpath='{.status.containerStatuses[0].state.waiting.reason}: {.status.containerStatuses[0].state.waiting.message}{"
"}'
CrashLoopBackOff: back-off 2m40s restarting failed container=crasher pod=lifecycle-crashloop_lifecycle(ac8db845-d7e4-43b6-8513-b8996acdc3c0)
# logs from the crashed instance, with --previous:

$ kubectl -n lifecycle logs lifecycle-crashloop --previous
starting up
crashing now

# Graceful termination again, this time reading the log during the delete
# so the SIGTERM handler's own output is captured.

$ kubectl -n lifecycle logs lifecycle-termination
app started

$ kubectl -n lifecycle logs -f lifecycle-termination   # streamed during the delete
app started
caught SIGTERM, draining for 10s
clean exit

# the delete took 11 seconds: the 10s drain, then exit

===== Done. Output saved to output-lifecycle.log =====
